# Copy this file to `.env` in this directory before starting the stack:
#
#     cp .env.example .env
#
# `.env` is gitignored and is NOT loaded from this example. Docker Compose
# auto-loads `.env` from the directory it is INVOKED FROM, on every `up`,
# regardless of your shell environment. This example exists so the setting is
# discoverable in version control; it does nothing on its own.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# REPO_PATH: the workspace root bound read-only at /workspace.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# It must be a PARENT of the repositories you register with
# `repocontext_add_repo`, so each repository is a child of the mount and can be
# registered at /workspace/<repo> (for example /workspace/lattice).
#
# WITHOUT THIS FILE, compose falls back to the built-in default declared in
# docker-compose.yml as `${REPO_PATH:-../../..}`. That default is this compose
# file's grandparent, which is correct ONLY when you invoke it from an ordinary
# clone: from C:\dev\lattice\samples\RepoContextContainer it resolves to C:\dev,
# whose registerable child is `lattice`.
#
# IT IS WRONG FROM A GIT WORKTREE, AND THE FAILURE IS SILENT. Invoked from
# C:\dev\copilot-worktrees\lattice\<worktree>\samples\RepoContextContainer, the
# same default resolves to the worktree COLLECTION directory, so the
# registerable child is the worktree's generated name rather than the
# repository's. The index then reports a healthy, converging repository under
# the expected id while every record in it describes a stale worktree. Nothing
# in any output surface distinguishes the two states.
#
# This has already happened once in this repository. See the local deployment
# runbook, section "What the box points at":
# docs/lattice.api.mcp.repocontext/local-deployment-runbook.md
#
# Set it to the parent directory that holds your checkouts.
REPO_PATH=C:\dev

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# REPOCONTEXT_CPUSET / EMBEDDER_CPUSET: opt-in CPU pinning (issue #2623).
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# UNSET BY DEFAULT, AND THAT IS THE SUPPORTED CONFIGURATION. Left unset, Compose
# omits the `cpuset` key from the resolved document entirely rather than
# emitting an empty one, so a stack that ignores these variables deploys exactly
# what it deployed before they existed.
#
# WHAT THEY DO. A container given a fractional CPU quota (`cpus: 4.0`, in
# docker-compose.tuning.yml) but no cpuset is ENTITLED to 4 CPUs while VISIBLE
# on all of the host's. CFS vends quota to per-CPU run queues in 5 ms slices and
# a thread waking on a queue draws a whole slice however briefly it runs, so
# threads scattered over many run queues exhaust the quota by RESERVATION rather
# than by execution and the kernel throttles the cgroup at a small fraction of
# its entitlement. Sizing the cpuset to the grant removes that: measured 5.4%
# throttled periods at ~17% quota utilisation with 16 CPUs visible, against
# 0.0% at ~26% utilisation pinned to 4, varying nothing else.
#
# HOW TO DERIVE VALUES. One range per service, sized to the CEILING of that
# service's `cpus` grant, and the two ranges MUST NOT OVERLAP or you trade
# throttling for contention. The values below are for the 16-CPU host the
# tuning overlay was measured on and are NOT portable - Docker refuses to start
# a container whose cpuset names a CPU the host does not have.
#
# WHEN NOT TO SET THEM. Never between a measurement run's T0 and its final
# scrape: a mid-run configuration change voids the run. Enable before a run
# starts, or not at all.
#
# REPOCONTEXT_CPUSET=0-5
# EMBEDDER_CPUSET=6-9

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# REPOCONTEXT_MEMORY_ARCHIVE_PATH: the host directory bound at /memory-archive.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# This is the ONLY copy of durable agent memory that survives
# `docker compose down -v`. /data holds the live tree and is exactly what that
# gesture destroys; this bind mount is not a declared volume, so it is not.
#
# IT HAS NO DEFAULT, AND THAT IS DELIBERATE. Compose resolves a relative bind
# source against the directory it was INVOKED FROM, and then CREATES it rather
# than refusing. The old default `./memory-archive` therefore followed the
# operator around, and the documented procedure composes from a candidate git
# WORKTREE on purpose - so the last surviving copy of the epic's memory was
# written into a tree that `git worktree remove`, a session cleanup, or a tidy
# of a copilot-worktrees collection deletes without warning.
#
# THE FAILURE IS SILENT, AND WORSE THAN THE REPO_PATH ONE ABOVE. A wrong
# REPO_PATH produces wrong answers, and wrong answers eventually surface. A
# wrong archive path produces a backup that is correct in every observable
# respect - written, rotating, current, and verifying by decoded content. Every
# check passes. It is invisible until the archive is needed, which is the moment
# no remedy remains. See issue #2627.
#
# SET IT TO AN ABSOLUTE PATH OUTSIDE EVERY GIT CHECKOUT AND WORKTREE. Not inside
# this repository, not inside a worktree of it, and not under the /data volume.
# Without it, every compose command in this directory - `up`, `down`, `ps`,
# `logs` - fails with a message naming this variable, which is the intended
# behaviour: a path nobody chose is the defect.
#
# Verify where it actually landed:
#
#     docker inspect repocontext --format '{{range .Mounts}}{{.Source}} -> {{.Destination}}{{"\n"}}{{end}}'
#     pwsh -File ./scripts/Assert-ContainerProvenance.ps1
#
# Check 5 of 5 in that script refuses an archive that resolved inside a git
# worktree or checkout. It keys on the ARCHIVE path only - never on the compose
# directory, which is legitimately a worktree and which check 2 of 5 requires to
# be one.
REPOCONTEXT_MEMORY_ARCHIVE_PATH=C:\dev\repocontext-memory-archive

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# DERIVED RESOURCE KNOBS (issue #2779) - DO NOT COPY THESE BETWEEN MACHINES.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Generate them, never hand-write them:
#
#     pwsh -File ./scripts/New-TuningEnv.ps1            # writes .env
#     pwsh -File ./scripts/New-TuningEnv.ps1 -DryRun    # prints, writes nothing
#
# docker-compose.tuning.yml references all seven as ${VAR:?...} with NO DEFAULT,
# so compose refuses every command and names the missing variable. That is
# deliberate. A default would be the bug this issue fixed wearing a variable's
# name: the values used to be literal transcriptions of one developer machine
# (16 logical CPUs, 55.7 GiB RAM), and the two memory limits summed to 17 GiB,
# so the stack could not start AT ALL on a 16 GiB host.
#
# WHAT DERIVES FROM WHAT. The memory grant is CORPUS-derived and host-CLAMPED,
# NOT a fraction of host RAM: the requirement is a property of the indexed
# repository, so a fixed fraction would over-grant on a big machine and
# under-grant on a small one for the identical corpus. The CPU grants ARE
# host-derived, because they are a share of a contended resource rather than a
# requirement. That 12g happened to be 21.5% of the reference box was a
# coincidence, and treating it as a rule is what made it unportable.
#
# The script REFUSES rather than granting less when the host cannot meet the
# corpus requirement. An under-grant does not present as a resource event: a
# 4 GiB cap on this service was never OOM-killed and `docker ps` reported it
# healthy throughout, while it emitted thousands of OutOfMemoryException lines
# from inside a grain-state READ path. It looks like a storage bug.
#
#   REPOCONTEXT_CPUS                    measurement-informed policy: share of
#                                       host logical CPUs (0.375 on reference)
#   REPOCONTEXT_MEM_LIMIT               DERIVED from corpus size, clamped by host
#   REPOCONTEXT_GC_HEAP_COUNT           DERIVED: equals REPOCONTEXT_CPUS
#   REPOCONTEXT_MAX_CONCURRENT_REPLAYS  DERIVED: equals REPOCONTEXT_CPUS (#2279)
#   EMBEDDER_CPUS                       policy: share of host logical CPUs (0.25)
#   EMBEDDER_MEM_LIMIT                  MEASURED: model-dominated, does not grow
#                                       with the repository
#   EMBEDDER_INTRA_THREADS              DERIVED: equals EMBEDDER_CPUS
#
# The last three of the repocontext group deriving from ONE number is the point.
# On the reference host they were three independent literals (6, 6, and 16) that
# agreed by hand where they had to agree and silently disagreed where it mattered.
